analysis:
The changes are limited to unloadModel in provider-swift/Sources/ProviderCore/ProviderLoop+ModelLoading.swift. At head 1133-1137, a successful Nemotron Super slot retirement now calls selectedModelRecovery.makeAllDue(now: .now). At head ~1204, a successful normal unload makes the same call after retryReserveDeferredPrefetches.

Effect: waiting selected-model recovery entries become due at the next capacity tick. This is a local scheduling and availability nudge (A-018). It is not caller-controlled input. The calls run only after successful retirement or unload, when the slot has already been removed and memory reclaimed. Load admission still goes through the existing paths: evictUntilAvailable, fastAdmissionReject, the post-load headroom guards (base 729, 915), throwIfRetiring, and the add-on hold drain owner (base 1191).

The rest of the unit text is unchanged base context and was reviewed only for context. That context includes:
- the TOCTOU weight-hash bracketing for cacheEligibleWeightHash (T-041/T-027, base 616-662);
- the GPU Metal requirement;
- the KV guards.

The PR does not touch any of these. No change was found to encryption, attestation, cache binding, authorization or the coordinator wire contract.

One question for integration: selectedModelRecovery's implementation is not in these units. Integration should confirm that making entries due cannot bypass the following:
- the add-on hold (TB-012/T-059), i.e. reloading models while an addonHold drain is active;
- the retirement refusal;
- the eviction-pin checks.

The risk is a recovery loop that reloads an unloaded model and churns it (a DoS against A-018). Nothing in this evidence shows that it does.
